-- WHY `let rec` CAN'T RIDE let-generalization TODAY (the #120 "say precisely why" ask).
-- A NON-recursive generic (`id`) ALREADY generalizes (ADR-0075 bite-0b): used at TWO types
-- (`Int` and `Unit+Unit`) in one program — no clash. Observed: `bang run` → 5.
-- The difference for `let rec`: the self-reference `$length` is bound in the body at a SINGLE
-- fixed monomorphic thunk type `uT` (TypeCheck.lean:3034), NOT a scheme — the μ-knot `buildLetRec`
-- closes over a CONCRETE `t'`. Generalizing the ascription would demand the recursive CALL
-- instantiate at a fresh type = POLYMORPHIC recursion (undecidable, R6 §4 wall). But the List
-- family is UNIFORM (`$length t` uses `a` at the SAME type) = MONOMORPHIC recursion, which
-- monomorphization handles by construction (w0/w3). So `let rec`'s barrier is not "no
-- generalization" but "the SELF-KNOT is monomorphic"; the fix is to close the tyvar BEFORE the
-- knot (per call-site instantiation), never to generalize the knot itself.
let id = {fun x => x}
let a = ($id) 5 in
let b = ($id) (Left(0)) in
a
